From: HONOR MagicBook Pro 14 AI (ZQC-P/M1010) Linux port
Subject: [PATCH] HID: multitouch: do not export vendor-defined application
 collections

The FocalTech FTSC1000 touchscreen (I2C HID 2808:5662) fitted to HONOR
MagicBook laptops declares, besides its Win8 digitizer collections, a
vendor-defined application collection on usage page 0xff01:

    Usage Page (0xff01)
    Usage (0x01)
    Collection (Application)
        Report ID (0x10)
        Report Size (8)
        Report Count (0x3b)
        Usage (0x01)
        Input (Data,Var,Abs)
    End Collection

It is a 59 byte raw data channel. But 0xff01 is also HID_UP_HPVENDOR2,
the page HP uses for its hotkey buttons, and hid-input maps usage
0xff010001 to KEY_MICMUTE unconditionally:

    case HID_UP_HPVENDOR2:
            set_bit(EV_REP, input->evbit);
            switch (usage->hid & HID_USAGE) {
            case 0x001: map_key_clear(KEY_MICMUTE);         break;

The device matches MT_CLS_WIN_8, which sets export_all_inputs, so the
collection is exported. With HID_QUIRK_INPUT_PER_APP that produces an
extra input device whose only capability is KEY_MICMUTE, fed by 59
eight-bit data fields that all carry the same usage. Any non-zero byte
in a vendor report is a key press; because hid-input also sets EV_REP
for this page, one such report leaves KEY_MICMUTE held down and
auto-repeating at ~30 Hz for the rest of the uptime.

The user-visible effect is a microphone that mutes and unmutes on its
own and a mic-mute LED that flickers. Measured on a HONOR MagicBook Pro
14 AI (ZQC-P/M1010): 29 KEY_MICMUTE events per second, continuously,
with no user interaction.

A vendor-defined application collection is by definition not portable
input; only the vendor's own driver can interpret it. hid-multitouch
should not export it, whatever export_all_inputs says. The one existing
exception, the Asus custom media keys collection, is preserved.

--- a/drivers/hid/hid-multitouch.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-multitouch.c
@@ -1502,6 +1502,25 @@
 		return -1;

 	/*
+	 * Vendor-defined application collections carry raw firmware or
+	 * diagnostic data, not input. Exporting them is actively harmful,
+	 * because hid-input maps a few vendor usage pages onto real keys:
+	 * page 0xff01 is HP's hotkey page, where usage 0x01 is KEY_MICMUTE.
+	 * A device that uses the same page for anything else therefore turns
+	 * every vendor report into phantom key events.
+	 *
+	 * Seen on the FocalTech FTSC1000 (2808:5662) touchscreen fitted to
+	 * HONOR MagicBook laptops: its 0xff01 collection is a 59 byte data
+	 * channel, and because MT_CLS_WIN_8 sets export_all_inputs it was
+	 * exported as a keyboard whose only key was KEY_MICMUTE, left
+	 * permanently held down and auto-repeating at ~30 Hz.
+	 */
+	if ((field->application & HID_USAGE_PAGE) >= HID_UP_MSVENDOR &&
+	    !(field->application == HID_VD_ASUS_CUSTOM_MEDIA_KEYS &&
+	      application->quirks & MT_QUIRK_ASUS_CUSTOM_UP))
+		return -1;
+
+	/*
 	 * Some Asus keyboard+touchpad devices have the hotkeys defined in the
 	 * touchpad report descriptor. We need to treat these as an array to
 	 * map usages to input keys.
